  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Thursday)\nIssued at 1121 AM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nWestern CONUS upper ridge will shift east into the Northern \nPlains and support hot and dry weather as a stronger upper trough \nmoves into the Pac NW. Weak shortwave advecting into the FA today\nand will merge with the deeper trough over the upper Midwest. \nLingering moisture and weak CIN will allow a few showers and \nisolated TS to develop across the western third this afternoon, \nspecifically northeast WY, the southern BH, and maybe far NW SD. \nLack of appreciable CAPE and insufficient deep layer shear will \nsupport weak pulse convection. Hence, severe weather is not \nexpected other than gusty winds. Convection will quickly wane this\nevening. Thermal ridge will slowly shift east ahead of the \nadvancing NW Pac upper trough, supporting hot conds Sat and Sun. \nThe warmest day will be Sunday when highs will reach the lower \n100s at many locations on the Plains. A heat adv may be needed on \nportions of the SD Plains Sunday as heat index readings will \nreach 100-105 in some areas. Lee-side trough will settle into the \nFA Sunday. However, cooler air won't advect into the FA until \nMonday when highs will be in the 80s and low 90s, coolest NW. \nStronger cold front will arrive Monday night, which will really \nknock temps down for Tue. This system may also support some \nconvection Monday night as the entrance region of the upper jet \nwill push through the region coupled with deep layer ascent and \nsufficient moisture. Highs will mainly be in the 70s Tue, the \nfirst below normal day in long while. Western ridge will flatten \nas a deep upper low slowly pushes east across interior Canada. \nThis will support a slow warm up as SE return flow ensues. \nIncreasing LL moisture will support isolated TS chances by Wed as \nweak impulses advect through the region in NW flow.",
